import json
from collections import Counter
T81='<table class="qt"><tr><th></th><th>Text 1</th><th>Text 2</th><th>Text 3</th><th>Text 4</th><th>Text 5</th></tr><tr><td>Predicted</td><td>7</td><td>7</td><td>6</td><td>5</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td>Actual</td><td>%s</td></tr></table><p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">%s</p>'
def t81(row,note): return T81%('</td><td>'.join(row),note)
Q1=[
("def","srldef","Which description best matches how self-regulated learning is defined in the handbook chapter on self-regulation and learning (Schunk &amp; Zimmerman)?",
 ["Learning that results from a student's own thoughts and behaviours, systematically aimed at her learning goals","Learning that takes place without help from teachers, parents or peers, so that the student works alone","A stable personality trait that separates disciplined students from impulsive students in every subject","The ability to resist distractions in the classroom when the teacher is not watching what the students are doing"],
 "Look for the option with three parts: self-generated, systematic, and aimed at goals.",
 "Zimmerman's (2000) definition: learning that results from students' <b>self-generated thoughts and behaviours</b> that are <b>systematically oriented</b> toward attaining their learning goals. Self-regulation does <b>not</b> mean social independence: seeking help is itself a self-regulatory process. Resisting distraction is one part, not the definition."),
("find","srldef","Judge these two statements about the features that theories of self-regulation share, according to the chapter on self-regulation and learning.<br><b>I.</b> Self-regulation is described as cyclical, made of feedback loops: set goals, monitor, respond.<br><b>II.</b> The theories look only at what learners do, not at why they choose to regulate themselves.",
 ["Only statement I is true","Only statement II is true","Both statements are true","Neither statement is true"],
 "Two shared features are tested; one of them is reversed.",
 "The theories share three features: learners are <b>active</b> (behaviourally, metacognitively, motivationally), the process is <b>cyclical</b> (feedback loops), and the theories address <b>why</b> people choose to self-regulate, including regulating motivation and emotion. Statement II reverses the third feature."),
("fig","theo5","The table summarizes the key features of self-regulation theories in the chapter on self-regulation and learning. Which entry belongs in the empty cell?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Key features</th></tr><tr><td>Operant</td><td>Self-monitoring, self-instruction, self-reinforcement</td></tr><tr><td>Information processing</td><td>Cognitive processes (attention, coding, storage, retrieval, metacognition); learning strategies</td></tr><tr><td>Developmental</td><td><b>?</b></td></tr><tr><td>Social constructivist</td><td>Theories of self-identity, tasks and strategies; cultural tools; zone of proximal development</td></tr></table><p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">After Table 3.1 (Schunk &amp; Zimmerman, 2012, p. 46), our wording.</p>',
 ["Improvements in cognitive processes with development; private speech","Reciprocal interactions of personal, behavioural and environmental factors","Discriminative stimuli and reinforcement contingencies set by the learner","Comparing judgments of comprehension with scores on inference tests"],
 "The row name tells you the lens: what changes as children grow older?",
 "Table 3.1 gives developmental theory <b>improvements in cognitive processes with development</b> and <b>private speech</b>. Reciprocal interactions belong to <b>social cognitive</b> theory, and reinforcement contingencies to <b>operant</b> theory. The last option describes the method of metacomprehension research in reading."),
("app","oper","Ella, an adult violin student, practises every day. At the end of each week in which she practised daily, she lets herself go to the cinema. What is the cinema trip, in operant terms?",
 ["Self-reinforcement","Self-instruction","Self-monitoring","A discriminative stimulus"],
 "A consequence she gives herself after the behaviour, to make it more likely: which process is that?",
 "Rewarding oneself contingent on a response, so the response becomes more likely, is <b>self-reinforcement</b>. A fixed practice time and place would be <b>discriminative stimuli</b> (cues). <b>Self-monitoring</b> would be deliberately attending to and recording the behaviour, and <b>self-instruction</b> would be statements that direct it."),
("find","oper","What does the chapter on self-regulation and learning conclude about self-reinforcement compared with reinforcement given by a teacher?",
 ["It is unclear whether self-reinforcement is more effective, because studies struggle to isolate its effect","Self-reinforcement is clearly more effective, because students value rewards they choose for themselves","Teacher reinforcement is clearly more effective, because students reward themselves too easily","Both are equally effective, as shown in a meta-analysis of classroom studies"],
 "A comparison the chapter calls unresolved.",
 "The chapter says it is <b>unclear</b> whether self-reinforcement beats externally administered reinforcement; studies often contain problems that make its isolated effect hard to discern (in class, students may stay on task because of the teacher's control). The reinforcement may matter more than its agent, and explicit reinforcement may matter most while skills are being learned."),
("comp","oper","Which criticism of the operant approach to self-regulation does the chapter on self-regulation and learning make?",
 ["By focusing only on behaviour, it ignores cognitive and motivational aspects such as beliefs and emotions","It explains long-term goals such as a degree well, but fails to change on-task behaviour in the short term","It relies too much on private speech, which young children rarely produce in class","It assumes that every learner develops self-regulation in four fixed social levels"],
 "The critique names what the operant lens leaves out.",
 "Focusing only on behaviour, operant theory <b>ignores cognitive and motivational aspects</b> and defines motivation as rate or duration of behaviour. The chapter adds that behavioural methods work in the <b>short term</b>, while long-term goals need a more elaborate perspective, which reverses the second option."),
("def","ip","In Winne and Hadwin's information processing model, a student interprets the teacher's written instructions for an essay. Which kind of information is this?",
 ["Task conditions","Cognitive conditions","Studying tactics","Adaptations"],
 "Where does the information come from: the external environment or long-term memory?",
 "<b>Task conditions</b> are information about the task interpreted from the external environment, such as the teacher's directions. <b>Cognitive conditions</b> come from long-term memory: how one did on earlier work, and motivational variables such as perceived competence. Studying tactics and adaptations are later phases of the model."),
("app","devcon","Lotte (6) says the right rule aloud while sorting shapes (\"first all the red ones\"), but her sorting does not follow what she says. Which deficiency does the developmental account describe here?",
 ["A mediational deficiency","A production deficiency","A continued-use deficiency","A calibration deficiency"],
 "She does produce the verbalization. Does it steer her behaviour?",
 "A <b>mediational deficiency</b> occurs when task-relevant verbalizations are produced but do not affect behaviour. A <b>production deficiency</b> is failing to generate useful verbalizations at all. The chapter also discusses students who stop using a strategy they were taught; calibration is not one of the deficiencies."),
("find","devcon","Vygotsky hypothesized that overt private speech increases until about ages 6 to 7 and then becomes mainly internal. What did later research reported in the chapter on self-regulation and learning add?",
 ["The amount of private speech falls from about 4 or 5 to 8, but the share that is self-regulating rises","Private speech keeps increasing until adolescence and becomes more self-regulating, contrary to what Vygotsky expected","Children who talk to themselves aloud learn less than children who stay silent","Private speech disappears completely by age 5 in nearly all children"],
 "The finding has two parts that move in opposite directions.",
 "Research shows that the <b>amount</b> of private speech decreases from about ages 4 or 5 to 8, while the <b>proportion that is self-regulating increases</b> with age (Fuson, 1979). Many children verbalize little or not at all, so the pattern seems more complex than Vygotsky hypothesized."),
("app","devcon","Mo cannot yet solve two-step equations on his own, but he can with a few hints from his teacher. In Vygotsky's terms, where is this task?",
 ["In his zone of proximal development","Beyond his zone, so it should be postponed","Below his level, so help is not needed","In his zone of self-reaction"],
 "Can do with help, cannot yet do alone.",
 "Tasks a student cannot do alone but can do with some assistance fall into the <b>zone of proximal development</b>. Scaffolding helps the learner operate increasingly independently as she internalizes the learning, after which the zone shifts. Self-reaction is a subfunction from social cognitive theory, not a zone."),
("fig","sct","College novices learning free throws were taught self-regulation for one, two or three phases. Which outcome matches the study as reported in the chapter on self-regulation and learning?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Group</th><th>Taught to</th></tr><tr><td>One phase</td><td>set goals</td></tr><tr><td>Two phases</td><td>set goals and self-record</td></tr><tr><td>Three phases</td><td>set goals, self-record, make attributions and adjust strategies after misses</td></tr></table><p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">Our table, after Cleary, Zimmerman &amp; Keating (2006).</p>',
 ["Three phases: the most adaptive attributions; two and three phases: the most accurate shooting","One phase: the most accurate shooting, because goals alone focus attention best of all","All three groups shot equally well; only their attributions differed afterwards","Two phases: the most adaptive attributions; three phases: the least accurate shooting"],
 "Two outcomes: attributions and accuracy. Which groups led on each?",
 "The three-phase group showed the <b>most adaptive attributions and strategy corrections</b>; the <b>two- and three-phase</b> groups shot most accurately (Cleary et al., 2006). The message: teaching goal setting alone (forethought) is not enough; self-regulation in all phases has desirable effects."),
("find","proc","Children practised writing paragraphs after a modeled strategy, with a process goal (learn to use the strategy), a product goal (write paragraphs) or a general goal (do your best); half the process-goal children also got progress feedback. Which condition was most effective?",
 ["The process goal with progress feedback","The process goal without progress feedback","The product goal, which was easy to check","The general goal, which caused the least pressure"],
 "Goal type plus feedback: which combination came out on top?",
 "In Schunk and Swartz (1993), the <b>process goal plus feedback</b> condition was the most effective, outperforming product and general goals on self-efficacy, writing achievement, self-evaluated progress and strategy use; the process goal alone gave some benefits. Gains were maintained after 6 weeks and transferred to untaught paragraph types."),
("find","proc","Girls learning dart throwing had a process goal, a product goal, or a shifting goal (process steps first, then a score once the steps were automatic). Which condition led to the highest self-efficacy and performance?",
 ["A shifting goal: the steps first, then a score","A product goal with a higher score target","Doing their best, without any set goal","A reversed shift: a score first, then the steps"],
 "The best condition changed the goal at a certain moment. In which order?",
 "In Zimmerman and Kitsantas (1997), the <b>shifting goal</b> led to the highest self-efficacy and performance. Process goals beat product goals, and self-recording helped too. The shift was designed to move learners from the self-control level (following the steps) to the self-regulation level (adapting to reach a score)."),
("comp","proc","Children who had previously failed at fractions watched either a peer coping model or a peer mastery model. What does the chapter on self-regulation and learning report, and why?",
 ["Coping models helped more, perhaps because learners with little task familiarity feel more similar to them","Mastery models helped more, because flawless performance shows learners the correct standard to imitate in their own work","Both helped equally, because peer models of any kind raise self-efficacy by the same amount","Neither helped, because only adult models raise self-efficacy and achievement"],
 "The children had not succeeded at fractions before: which model would they feel similar to?",
 "Schunk, Hanson and Cox (1987) found that peer <b>coping</b> models enhanced self-efficacy and achievement more than peer mastery models with fractions, a task the children had not mastered. <b>Coping models may be more effective</b> when students have little familiarity or earlier difficulties. In an earlier study, peer models beat adult and no models."),
("find","proc","Children with learning disabilities learned subtraction while verbalizing the steps aloud throughout, stopping halfway, or not at all. Which result does the chapter on self-regulation and learning report?",
 ["Continuous verbalization led to the highest self-efficacy and achievement","Stopping halfway led to the highest achievement, because speech became internal","No verbalization led to the highest achievement, because talking distracts","All three conditions produced the same self-efficacy and achievement"],
 "Three conditions of talking aloud; which kept the advantage?",
 "In Schunk and Cox (1986), <b>continuous verbalization</b> led to the highest self-efficacy and achievement. Students told to stop may not have kept using the verbal mediators. The trap is assuming that speech going inward always helps."),
("def","subf","Sara compares her essay grade with the personal best she set last term. Which subfunction of self-regulation is this, in social cognitive theory?",
 ["Self-evaluation","Self-observation","Self-reaction","Forethought"],
 "Comparing with a standard is the key move.",
 "Judging oneself against a standard is <b>self-evaluation</b> (self-judgment); personal bests are self-modeled standards that motivate. <b>Self-observation</b> is noticing one's thoughts and behaviour, and <b>self-reaction</b> is responding, for example with a reward. Forethought is a phase of the cycle, not a subfunction."),
("app","subf","Marta, a manager, sees in her calendar that most evenings go to email instead of the language course she enrolled in, yet she changes nothing. Which explanation fits the social cognitive chapter by Usher and Schunk?",
 ["Self-observation alone is not reliably related to self-regulation; control and value also matter","Marta lacks self-observation, so making her more aware will solve the problem","Marta has internalized standards that are too high, so she gives up","Marta is still in the forethought phase of the cycle, which never includes any self-observation at all"],
 "She is aware. What else must be in place before awareness leads to change?",
 "Like the chapter's gaming teenager, she is <b>aware</b> of the problem; change also depends on whether she feels it is under her <b>control</b> and whether she <b>values</b> the activity. Whether self-observation leads to change depends on timing, controllability, value and motivation: <b>self-observation alone is not reliably related</b> to self-regulation."),
("fig","cyc","The table redraws Figure 2.1 of the social cognitive chapter (Usher &amp; Schunk). Which entry belongs in the empty cell?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Part</th><th>Items</th></tr><tr><td>Forethought</td><td>Task analysis</td><td>Planning · goal setting · visualization</td></tr><tr><td>Performance</td><td>Strategy use</td><td>Note taking · help-seeking · self-talk</td></tr><tr><td>Reflection</td><td>Assessment</td><td><b>?</b></td></tr><tr><td>Reflection</td><td>Self-reaction</td><td>Rewards/sanctions · emotional response · revision of goals</td></tr></table><p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">After Figure 2.1 (Usher &amp; Schunk, 2017, p. 22).</p>',
 ["Causal attribution · strategic review","Self-efficacy · outcome expectations","Monitoring of one's efforts · self-talk","Planning · visualization · goal setting"],
 "Assessment happens after the task: what do people look at then?",
 "In Figure 2.1 the reflection phase's <b>assessment</b> holds <b>causal attribution</b> and <b>strategic review</b>. Self-efficacy and outcome expectations belong to forethought (motivation and beliefs), and monitoring of efforts to performance."),
("app","cyc","Before joining a choir, Hana asks herself two questions: \"Can I sing these difficult harmonies?\" and \"Can I keep practising three evenings a week next to my job?\" In social cognitive terms, which beliefs is she judging?",
 ["Self-efficacy for the skill and self-efficacy for self-regulation","Outcome expectations and implicit theory of ability","Self-evaluation and self-reaction","Task value and interest in the topic"],
 "Two can-I questions: one about the skill, one about managing herself.",
 "In the forethought phase people may judge their efficacy for several things: whether she can sing the harmonies is self-efficacy for the <b>skill</b> (the chapter's parallel is reading self-efficacy for a long novel); whether she can set aside the time and attention is <b>self-efficacy for self-regulation</b>. Outcome expectations concern what an action will lead to, not whether one can do it."),
("comp","cyc","Judge these two statements about Zimmerman's cyclical model as presented by Usher and Schunk.<br><b>I.</b> Self-observation, self-evaluation and self-reaction are involved in each of the three phases.<br><b>II.</b> One cycle always covers a single study session.",
 ["Only statement I is true","Only statement II is true","Both statements are true","Neither statement is true"],
 "One statement places the subfunctions; the other sets the time scale.",
 "The three <b>subfunctions</b> are involved at <b>each of the three phases</b> (Figure 2.1). The cycle applies to large and small events, so it can take <b>minutes, days or years</b> (Zimmerman, 2011): statement II is false."),
("app","dev3","Kim writes lab reports alone using exactly the template his teacher modeled. When a report needs a different structure, he is stuck. At which level of the social-self progression is he?",
 ["Self-control","Self-regulation","Emulation","Observation"],
 "He works alone, but does he adapt?",
 "At the <b>self-control</b> level learners use the strategy independently but still follow the model's representational patterns. At the <b>self-regulation</b> level they systematically adapt to changing personal and situational conditions. <b>Emulation</b> is practising the modeled behaviour with feedback, still guided by the model."),
("def","agency","Within the school she must attend, Lena starts an online study group with a shared planner. According to social cognitive theory, what kind of environment has she made?",
 ["A created environment","An imposed environment","A selected environment","A reciprocal environment"],
 "Three kinds: put on you, chosen within it, or built by you.",
 "Students can <b>create</b> learning environments with physical, social or technological resources, which requires a high degree of agentic control (Bandura, 1986). School itself is <b>imposed</b>; choosing classes or friends inside it is a <b>selected</b> environment."),
("comp","sct","Operant theory and social cognitive theory both include monitoring one's own behaviour. What does the social cognitive view add?",
 ["Personal factors such as self-efficacy and standards, interacting with behaviour and environment","Reinforcement contingencies, which the operant view leaves entirely to the teacher and the classroom rules","Private speech as the main mechanism of all self-regulation","A focus on behaviour alone, so that results are easier to measure"],
 "Which lens includes what is going on in the person?",
 "Social cognitive theory explains self-regulation through <b>reciprocal interactions</b> of personal, behavioural and environmental factors; self-observation feeds self-evaluation against <b>standards</b>, and <b>self-efficacy</b> drives it. The operant view, by focusing on behaviour only, ignores these cognitive and motivational aspects."),
("app","srsd","In a writing programme, the teacher and pupils write a story together using the planning mnemonic and a graphic organizer, and check that all seven story parts are in it. Which stage of Self-Regulated Strategy Development is this?",
 ["Support it","Model it","Discuss it","Independent performance"],
 "Who does the writing here: the teacher alone, together, or the pupils alone?",
 "Writing <b>together</b> with mnemonics and graphic organizers is the <b>support it</b> stage. In <b>model it</b> the teacher demonstrates the strategy with self-statements; in <b>independent performance</b> pupils write alone. Responsibility shifts gradually from teacher to student as scaffolding fades."),
("comp","theo5","A pupil talks herself through each step of a sum out loud. Which two theories in the chapter on self-regulation and learning give such private speech a central place?",
 ["Developmental theory and social constructivist theory","Operant theory and information processing theory","Information processing theory and social cognitive theory","Operant theory and social cognitive theory"],
 "Which theories treat self-talk as something that develops and becomes internal?",
 "<b>Developmental</b> theory (for example Luria's stages of verbal control) and Vygotsky's <b>social constructivist</b> theory both give private speech a central role. Self-instruction statements also appear in operant theory, but there they are cues for reinforcement, not a developmental mechanism."),
]
Q2=[
("def","acc","In research on metacomprehension, what is relative accuracy?",
 ["Whether a reader's judgments tell apart the texts she understood better and worse","Whether a reader's average judgment matches her average test score across texts","Whether a reader's test scores are higher than those of other readers in her class","Whether a reader's judgments stay the same when she rereads the same text later"],
 "Relative to what: to other readers, or to her own other texts?",
 "<b>Relative accuracy</b> is the extent to which judgments discriminate between better and less understood texts, measured as an intra-individual correlation across texts. Matching the averages is <b>absolute accuracy</b> (confidence bias). It has nothing to do with comparing readers."),
("fig","acc","A student predicted her scores on five texts and then took the tests. What best describes her accuracy?"+t81(['4','6','5','7','6'],'Illustrative values, scores out of 7, in the format of Table 8.1 (Thiede &amp; de Bruin, 2017, p. 125).'),
 ["No confidence bias, but poor relative accuracy","Over-confident, but good relative accuracy","Under-confident, and good relative accuracy","No bias, and perfect relative accuracy"],
 "First compare the averages (bias), then compare the order of the texts.",
 "Average predicted is 28/5 = 5.6 and average actual is 28/5 = 5.6: <b>no confidence bias</b>. But she rated texts 1 and 2 highest and text 5 lowest, while she scored lowest on text 1 and among the highest on text 5: her ranking runs largely against her scores, so <b>relative accuracy is poor</b>, like Student 3 in Table 8.1. Absolute and relative accuracy are independent."),
("fig","acc","In Table 8.1 of the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading, four students made the same predictions (7, 7, 6, 5, 3). Which student combines a confidence bias of +3 with a relative accuracy of +1.0?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Student</th><th>Actual scores on texts 1 to 5</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>7, 7, 6, 5, 3</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>4, 4, 3, 2, 0</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>3, 5, 6, 7, 7</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>0, 2, 3, 4, 4</td></tr></table><p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">After Table 8.1 (Thiede &amp; de Bruin, 2017, p. 125); labels A to D are ours.</p>',
 ["Student B","Student A","Student C","Student D"],
 "Bias +3: every score 3 lower on average. Relative +1.0: the same order as the predictions.",
 "Student B scored exactly 3 below each prediction: <b>bias +3</b>, but the order matches perfectly: <b>relative accuracy +1.0</b>. A is perfect on both; C has no bias but a reversed order (−1.0); D is poor on both (+3, −1.0)."),
("find","acc","According to the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading, how does domain knowledge relate to the two kinds of metacomprehension accuracy?",
 ["It influences absolute accuracy but not relative accuracy","It influences relative accuracy but not absolute accuracy","It influences both kinds of accuracy to the same degree","It influences neither kind once reading skill is controlled"],
 "This finding is the reason to keep the two kinds apart.",
 "Griffin, Jee and Wiley (2009) showed that <b>domain knowledge</b> influences <b>absolute</b> but not <b>relative</b> accuracy. Because a variable can affect one kind and not the other, researchers must say which kind they study; the chapter focuses on relative accuracy."),
("comp","cue","Why are readers' judgments of comprehension often inaccurate, according to the combination of the cue-utilization framework and the construction-integration model?",
 ["Judgments are inferred from cues, and many readers use cues that do not reflect the situation model","Readers can inspect their comprehension directly but report it carelessly","Comprehension tests measure the surface level of the text, while readers base their judgments on the situation model","Readers judge from the situation model, which is unrelated to test performance"],
 "One theory says how judgments are made; the other says what the test depends on.",
 "Judgments are <b>inferential</b>: based on available cues (Koriat, 1997). Comprehension tests depend largely on the <b>situation model</b> (Kintsch, 1988), so cues from that level are diagnostic. Readers who rely on surface cues, such as ease of reading, judge poorly. The third and fourth options reverse the logic."),
("find","cue","College students reported which cues they used to judge their comprehension. Which students had the highest relative accuracy, according to Thiede et al. (2010)?",
 ["Those who relied on their ability to understand or explain the text","Those who relied on how easy or short the text was","Those who relied on their interest in the topic","Those who relied on how much prior knowledge of the topic they already had"],
 "Which cue type is closest to what a comprehension test asks?",
 "Accuracy was greater for students who relied on <b>comprehension-based cues</b> (their ability to understand or explain the text) than for students using other cues, and lower for those relying on <b>surface features</b> such as difficulty, length or vocabulary."),
("def","cue","In Kintsch's construction-integration model, which level of text representation connects information from the text with the reader's prior knowledge to draw inferences?",
 ["The situation model","The textbase","The lexical or surface level","The production system"],
 "The deepest of the three levels.",
 "The <b>situation model</b> is built when text information is connected to prior knowledge and used to make inferences and implications. The <b>textbase</b> consists of propositions linked by explicit text features, and the <b>surface level</b> holds the words and phrases. A production system belongs to information processing accounts in reading 1."),
("find","ret","Students read six texts. One group summarized each text right after reading it, another read all six texts first and then summarized them, and a control group wrote no summaries. Which group judged its comprehension most accurately?",
 ["The delayed-summary group","The immediate-summary group","The control group","All three groups were equally accurate"],
 "Timing of the retrieval is the manipulation.",
 "In Thiede and Anderson (2003), accuracy was greater for the <b>delayed-summary</b> group than for the other groups. Right after reading, much is still active in working memory even for poorly understood texts, so immediate retrieval gives cues that do not tell the texts apart."),
("comp","ret","How do activation theories explain the effect of the timing of a retrieval task on metacomprehension accuracy?",
 ["After a delay only long-term memory is available, so poorly understood texts yield little","After a delay readers are less tired and can concentrate better on the judgment","After a delay readers have forgotten their first impression of the text, so they guess less often","After a delay the textbase is stronger, so the cues become more homogeneous"],
 "What has faded after a delay, and what is left?",
 "Immediately after reading, information is still active in short-term memory, giving <b>homogeneous</b> cues for well and poorly understood texts. After a delay, only <b>long-term memory</b> remains: poorly understood texts yield little, well understood texts much more. These <b>heterogeneous</b> cues match what is available at the later test."),
("find","ret","Researchers varied the time between reading and writing keywords, and between writing keywords and judging comprehension. Which delay turned out to be crucial (Thiede, Dunlosky, Griffin &amp; Wiley, 2005)?",
 ["The delay between reading and generating keywords","The delay between generating keywords and judging","The delay between judging and taking the test","None: only the number of keywords mattered"],
 "Which gap lets surface information fade before retrieval?",
 "The <b>delay between reading and keyword generation</b> was the crucial ingredient: generating keywords once textbase information had faded produced a robust positive effect on accuracy."),
("app","ret","A primary school wants all pupils, from grade 4 upward, to write keywords about each text before judging which texts to reread. What does the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading suggest about age?",
 ["Effects are reported down to grade 6, but retrieval tasks may not work in grade 4","Delayed keywords work at every age, from grade 1 onward, as long as pupils can write","Delayed keywords only work for college students","Immediate keywords would work better for young pupils"],
 "Find the youngest grade where the effect was shown.",
 "The retrieval effects appear robust for typical and at-risk college students and for students as young as <b>6th grade</b>, but such tasks <b>may not be effective</b> for students as young as <b>4th grade</b> (de Bruin et al., 2011)."),
("find","sit","Seventh graders either made concept maps while reading, were given ready-made concept maps to study, or had no maps. How accurate was the group that was given ready-made maps (Redford et al., 2012)?",
 ["No more accurate than the control group","As accurate as the group that made maps","More accurate than the group that made maps","More accurate than the control, less than the map makers"],
 "Making versus receiving: which part matters?",
 "Constructing maps improved accuracy, but students who examined <b>already-constructed maps</b> were <b>no more accurate than controls</b>. The act of <b>generating</b> the map appears to be crucial; the authors speculated it makes situation-model cues more salient."),
("comp","sit","Self-explaining while reading was compared with reading the texts twice (equal study time) and reading them once. Why was the read-twice group included?",
 ["To rule out that the benefit of self-explaining is only extra study time","To show that rereading is the best comprehension strategy","To test whether students prefer rereading to explaining","To measure absolute accuracy separately from relative accuracy"],
 "What would a critic say if self-explaining simply took longer?",
 "The read-twice group had study time <b>equivalent</b> to the self-explanation group. Self-explainers were still more accurate (Griffin, Wiley &amp; Thiede, 2008), so the advantage is not just time on task: self-explaining focuses readers on their situation model."),
("find","sit","After practice texts with either memory tests (facts) or inference tests (comprehension), students predicted their performance on new texts. What did Thiede, Griffin and Wiley (2011) find?",
 ["Memory-test expectancy raised metamemory accuracy; inference-test expectancy raised metacomprehension accuracy","Inference-test expectancy raised both kinds of accuracy, so memory tests are pointless","Memory-test expectancy raised both kinds of accuracy, because facts are easier to judge than understanding of a text","Neither expectancy changed accuracy; only the texts themselves mattered"],
 "Two expectancies, two kinds of accuracy: match them.",
 "<b>Metamemory</b> accuracy was greater for the group expecting <b>memory</b> tests, and <b>metacomprehension</b> accuracy for the group expecting <b>inference</b> tests. What students expect to be tested on shapes how they read and monitor."),
("def","reg","Which description matches the discrepancy-reduction model of study regulation?",
 ["Students keep studying until the gap between current and desired learning is zero, so harder material is restudied more","Students study the easiest material first, so they reach their goal with the least effort","Students reduce the gap between their own and other students' scores by comparing their results after each test","Students stop studying once the material feels fluent, whatever their goal"],
 "Discrepancy = gap. Which material has the larger gap?",
 "Students set a desired state of learning, monitor how well material is learned, and keep studying until the <b>discrepancy</b> reaches zero (Dunlosky &amp; Hertzog, 1997). Because the gap is larger for harder material, the model predicts that <b>less learned material</b> is selected for restudy more often."),
("find","reg","What was new about Thiede, Anderson and Therriault (2003) according to the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading?",
 ["It gave the first evidence that better accuracy leads to better restudy choices and better comprehension","It was the first study to measure relative accuracy with an intra-individual correlation between judgments and test scores","It showed that at-risk readers cannot regulate their study","It showed that summaries improve accuracy only when written immediately"],
 "The study linked three things in one chain.",
 "The delayed-keyword group judged more accurately, then used a discrepancy-reduction rule to choose <b>less learned texts</b> for rereading, and after rereading had better <b>overall comprehension</b>: the <b>first empirical evidence</b> that metacomprehension accuracy affects comprehension."),
("comp","rtch","Which conclusion for teachers does the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading draw?",
 ["Work on the accuracy of monitoring; even young students already regulate study well","Work on regulation; students monitor well but do not reread what they misunderstood","Work on both equally; children of all ages struggle with both monitoring and regulation","Do not intervene; both monitoring and regulation develop by themselves with age"],
 "Which link of the chain is weak, and which is in place early?",
 "Even young students allocate more time to harder material, so teachers <b>need not intervene</b> on regulation. <b>Inaccurate monitoring</b> is ubiquitous, even among college students, so teachers should focus readers on cues that predict the test: describe the test, assess deep comprehension, promote self-testing."),
("find","rtch","Students judged their comprehension of texts with uninformative pictures. What did Ackerman and Leiser (2014) find, as reported in the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading?",
 ["The harmful effect on monitoring was limited to lower-performing students","All students judged more accurately when pictures were added","Higher-performing students were misled most by the pictures","The pictures raised test scores but not judgments"],
 "For whom did decorative pictures cause trouble?",
 "Uninformative pictures raised judgments of comprehension without improving performance; Ackerman and Leiser (2014) showed this adverse effect was <b>limited to lower performers</b>, while higher performers even profited, perhaps because they worked harder to make sense of them."),
("find","rtch","Students read the same texts on paper or on screen. What did Ackerman and Goldsmith (2011) find?",
 ["Better calibration on paper; when study was self-paced, paper also led to better regulation and scores","Better calibration and better comprehension scores on screen, whatever the pacing of the study session was","No differences between paper and screen in calibration or scores","Better scores on screen, but better calibration on paper"],
 "Two comparisons: fixed time and self-paced.",
 "Calibration was <b>better on paper</b>, without a difference in comprehension; when study was <b>self-paced</b>, the better calibration on paper translated into <b>better regulation and higher test scores</b>. The authors conclude that students need extra support when studying on screen."),
("find","comp6","Undergraduates with low self-efficacy for self-regulation were compared with peers who had higher self-efficacy for self-regulation. What did Klassen, Krawchuk and Rajani (2008) find, as reported by Usher and Schunk?",
 ["They were more likely to procrastinate","They were more likely to seek help from teachers","They spent more time studying, but less effectively","They set more challenging goals to compensate"],
 "A belief about managing oneself, and a behaviour that undermines it.",
 "Students with <b>lower self-efficacy for self-regulation</b> were more likely to <b>procrastinate</b>. The chapter places this under the <b>behaviour</b> component, next to self-handicapping, which a meta-analysis of over 25,000 students linked to poorer academic performance."),
("app","comp6","Before an important exam, Yusuf goes out the night before and tells friends he \"did not really study\". Which concept from the social cognitive chapter fits, and why might he do it?",
 ["Self-handicapping, so a failure can be blamed on an external cause","Incubation, so that his creative ideas can mature","Co-regulation, so his friends share responsibility for his goals","Outcome expectation, because he expects the exam to be easy"],
 "He creates an obstacle in advance. What does that protect?",
 "Learners who fear failing expectations tend to <b>self-handicap</b>, which lets them attribute an expected failure to an <b>external</b> rather than internal cause (Urdan &amp; Midgley, 2001). A meta-analysis of over 25,000 students linked self-handicapping to poorer performance (Schwinger et al., 2014)."),
("comp","comp6","A very experienced pianist starts consciously monitoring every finger movement during a concert. What does the social cognitive chapter suggest about this?",
 ["Once a skill is proficient, heightened self-monitoring might impede performance","More self-monitoring always improves performance, at every level of skill","Self-monitoring matters only for experts, not for beginners","Monitoring has no effect once a skill is automatic"],
 "When is monitoring most needed, and when can it get in the way?",
 "The need for cognitive self-monitoring may be <b>highest while a skill is being developed</b>; after proficiency, heightened self-monitoring <b>might impede</b> performance (Mace et al., 2001). Experienced athletes are coached not to over-think, and artists value incubation."),
("app","comp6","Students who feel that people of their background are expected to do badly in maths spend effort managing that worry during a test. What does the social cognitive chapter say happens to their self-regulation?",
 ["Less cognitive power is left for regulating the task: emotion regulation costs cognitive regulation","They regulate the task better than other students, because the threat raises their effort and their attention to the task","Nothing changes if they are motivated enough","They switch to the self-control level of development"],
 "Where does the effort to manage a threat come from?",
 "In threatening environments, students who must expend effort mitigating the threat have <b>less cognitive power</b> for regulating their performance (Schmader, Johns &amp; Forbes, 2008): <b>emotion regulation comes at the expense of cognitive regulation</b>. A strong sense of efficacy can act as a buffer."),
("app","impl","A teacher wants to strengthen the self-efficacy of a pupil who doubts he can learn maths. Which move does the social cognitive chapter by Usher and Schunk single out as typically the strongest source of self-efficacy?",
 ["Structured opportunities for skill development and mastery","Telling him that maths is easy and that everyone can do it","Showing him the scores of the best students in the class","Letting him skip the topics he finds hardest this term"],
 "Of the sources of self-efficacy, which one rests on his own success?",
 "Teachers should structure opportunities for skill development and <b>mastery</b>, which typically serve as the <b>strongest source</b> of self-efficacy (Usher &amp; Pajares, 2008). Social models who overcame difficulty and feedback that highlights capability help as well."),
("comp","cmp3","Which pair of classroom practices follows from the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading and from the social cognitive chapter, respectively?",
 ["Reading 2: describe the test and promote delayed self-testing; social cognitive: build self-efficacy and self-set goals","Reading 2: train pupils to choose which texts to reread first; social cognitive: add external rewards for every finished task","Reading 2: give ready-made concept maps; social cognitive: let students observe flawless experts only","Reading 2: have pupils summarize right after each page; social cognitive: set all goals for the students"],
 "Match each reading's weak link with its practice.",
 "Reading 2 targets <b>monitoring</b>: say what the test assesses, assess deep comprehension, promote delayed self-testing. The social cognitive chapter targets <b>motivation</b>: build self-efficacy through mastery, models and feedback, and support self-set goals and incentives. The other options contradict findings in the readings."),
]

def setq(Q,i,s=None,q=None):
    x=list(Q[i])
    if s: x[0]=s
    if q: x[2]=q
    Q[i]=tuple(x)
CAP='<p style="font-size:.85rem;color:#5d6472;text-align:center;margin:0 0 6px">%s</p>'
setq(Q1,12,s="fig",q="Girls learning to throw darts were given different goals. Which condition belongs in the empty cell?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Goal condition</th><th>Self-efficacy and performance</th></tr><tr><td>Product goal (a score)</td><td>Lowest</td></tr><tr><td>Process goal (the steps)</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td><b>?</b></td><td>Highest</td></tr></table>'+CAP%'Our table, after Zimmerman &amp; Kitsantas (1997) as reported in Schunk &amp; Zimmerman (2012).')
setq(Q2,11,s="fig",q="Seventh graders read texts under three conditions and then judged their comprehension. Which result belongs in the empty cell?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th>Condition while reading</th><th>Metacomprehension accuracy</th></tr><tr><td>Constructed their own concept maps</td><td>Improved</td></tr><tr><td>Examined ready-made concept maps</td><td><b>?</b></td></tr><tr><td>No maps (control)</td><td>Baseline</td></tr></table>'+CAP%'Our table, after Redford et al. (2012) as reported by Thiede &amp; de Bruin (2017).')
setq(Q2,13,s="fig",q="After three practice texts, students expected either memory tests (facts) or inference tests (comprehension), and then predicted their performance on new texts. Which pattern of results is reported?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th></th><th>Metamemory accuracy</th><th>Metacomprehension accuracy</th></tr><tr><td>Expected memory tests</td><td><b>?</b></td><td><b>?</b></td></tr><tr><td>Expected inference tests</td><td><b>?</b></td><td><b>?</b></td></tr></table>'+CAP%'Our table, after Thiede, Griffin &amp; Wiley (2011) as reported by Thiede &amp; de Bruin (2017).')
setq(Q2,18,s="fig",q="Students read texts on paper or on screen, with a fixed study time or at their own pace. Which description fits the results reported in the chapter on self-regulated learning in reading?"+'<table class="qt"><tr><th></th><th>Calibration of judgments</th><th>Comprehension scores</th></tr><tr><td>Study not self-paced</td><td><b>?</b></td><td><b>?</b></td></tr><tr><td>Self-paced study</td><td><b>?</b></td><td><b>?</b></td></tr></table>'+CAP%'Our table, after Ackerman &amp; Goldsmith (2011) as reported by Thiede &amp; de Bruin (2017).')

def mk(Q):
    out=[]
    for q in Q:
        assert len(set(q[3]))==4,q[2]
        out.append({"s":q[0],"t":q[1],"q":q[2],"o":q[3],"a":0,"spot":q[4],"why":q[5]})
    assert len(out)==25,len(out)
    return out
S1,S2=mk(Q1),mk(Q2)
for name,S in (('1',S1),('2',S2)):
    longest=[i for i,d in enumerate(S) if len(d['o'][0])>max(map(len,d['o'][1:]))]
    print(name,Counter(d['s'] for d in S),'correct longest:',len(longest),longest)
    assert not any('—' in json.dumps(d,ensure_ascii=False) for d in S)
